<p>The US Supreme Court sounded skeptical on April 1 of President Donald Trump’s effort to narrow birthright citizenship, as justices across the ideological spectrum pressed the administration on whether an executive order can override the long-settled reading of the 14th Amendment. Trump attended the oral arguments in Washington, becoming the first sitting US president to do so and underscoring the importance his administration has placed on a case that could affect children born in the United States to parents who are undocumented or in the country on temporary visas. The administration argued that automatic citizenship has encouraged illegal immigration and what it called “birth tourism,” while opponents said the order conflicts with both the Constitution and more than a century of legal practice. A ruling is expected by June or July, and the court’s questioning offered an early sign that the constitutional debate will get close scrutiny as it moves through the judicial process.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/a7I9Qxr6WQ6-5gslLdjApZIyh7jTk3-r5gxnDqbJ-3-KvJDU88sOHV5ugB3Z5j0OgoYCY4k0IHcLvXieRB7pidkVIF97-cPEMSF1xdue92bk' alt='Demonstrators hold signs outside the US Supreme Court building on the day of oral arguments over the Trump administration’s effort to limit birthright citizenship in Washington, DC, on April 1, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Historic attendance: Clare Cushman of the Supreme Court Historical Society said Trump was the first sitting president to attend a Supreme Court oral argument, a rare break from modern protocol.</li><li>Courtroom exchange: When Solicitor General D. John Sauer argued that changing migration realities meant “we’re in a new world now,” Chief Justice John Roberts replied, “It’s a new world. It’s the same Constitution”.</li><li>Order scope: Trump’s order told federal agencies not to recognize citizenship for US-born children if neither parent is a US citizen or lawful permanent resident, extending beyond undocumented migrants to some temporary visa holders.</li><li>Public response: Protesters gathered outside the Supreme Court to oppose the proposed change, reflecting how personal and politically significant the issue is for immigrant communities and advocates.</li></ul><blockquote>It’s a new world. It’s the same Constitution. - Chief Justice John Roberts</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration: Solicitor General D. John Sauer argued that automatic birthright citizenship acts as a pull factor for illegal immigration and supports a broader birth tourism industry, and said most countries do not follow the same rule. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987575/us-supreme-court-justices-skeptical-of-trumps-order-to-restrict-birthright-citizenship'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Supreme Court justices: Several justices, conservative and liberal alike, questioned whether the administration could change the understanding of the 14th Amendment by executive order rather than through legislation or a constitutional amendment. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cje47jk49k0o'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Opponents and protesters: Demonstrators outside the court said the case hits close to home for families who see birthright citizenship as a core legal protection for children born in the United States. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/1/hits-close-to-home-us-supreme-court-hears-birthright-citizenship-case'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Chinese observers in debate: Coverage of the arguments highlighted that China was singled out in discussion of birth tourism, showing that the US constitutional dispute is also being watched through an international migration and diplomacy lens. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3348722/china-targeted-birthright-citizenship-debate-supreme-court-justices-sceptical'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Justice/2026/0401/supreme-court-birthright-citizenship-trump'>After more than 100 years of birthright citizenship, Supreme Court appears skeptical of change</a> - csmonitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/trump-makes-historic-supreme-court-appearance-limiting-birthright-citizenship-cas/article70811925.ece'>Whispers in Supreme Court as Trump takes front-row seat for oral arguments</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-supreme-court-debates-citizenship-with-rare-trump-visit/a-76630576'>US Supreme Court debates citizenship, with rare Trump visit</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cje47jk49k0o'>US Supreme Court appears sceptical of Trump plan to limit birthright citizenship</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/01/2026/us-supreme-court-signals-skepticism-of-trump-birthright-order'>US Supreme Court signals skepticism of Trump birthright order</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/01/trump-attends-supreme-court-hearing-on-ending-birthright-citizenship_6752023_4.html'>Trump attends Supreme Court hearing on ending birthright citizenship</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3348722/china-targeted-birthright-citizenship-debate-supreme-court-justices-sceptical'>China targeted in US birthright citizenship debate, but Supreme Court justices sceptical</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-us-supreme-court-seems-poised-to-reject-trumps-birthright-citizenship/'>U.S. Supreme Court seems poised to reject Trump&#x27;s birthright citizenship limits</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/04/01/us-supreme-court-casts-doubt-on-donald-trumps-bid-to-limit-birthright-citizenship'>US Supreme Court casts doubt on Donald Trump&#x27;s bid to limit birthright citizenship</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987575/us-supreme-court-j